{
  "0": "Sequence modeling: in all four datasets, halve the current maximum causal context length, rounding up, and retain the most recent interactions in chronological order. Apply identical truncation during training and prediction. Shorter histories may reduce stale-interest interference and recurrent computation. Preserve embeddings, GRU topology, readout, loss, optimizer and training settings; train one model per dataset.",
  "1": "Network architecture: in all four datasets, add one shared residual bottleneck block to each GRU output: h'=h+0.1*W2(GELU(W1(h))), with bottleneck width max(1, hidden_size//4) and W2 initialized to zero. Feed these states into the unchanged readout and scorer. This adds modest nonlinear capacity while initially preserving the original representation. Keep embeddings, histories, loss and optimizer unchanged; update the predictor writer to load this single network.",
  "2": "Learning objective: in all four datasets, use L_existing + 0.05*mean(relu(1+s_negative-s_positive)), computed only over existing valid training positions and already sampled negatives. The margin term may focus learning on insufficiently separated pairs and improve HR@10. Preserve sampling, features, embeddings, GRU, readout, scoring, optimizer, scheduler and batch/epoch settings. Each dataset retains one trained model.",
  "3": "Interest aggregation: in all four datasets, replace the existing readout r with 0.9*r+0.1*m, where m is the normalized recency-weighted mean of valid GRU states, with weights proportional to 2^(-age/5), age measured in interactions. This modest contribution from recent states may stabilize noisy terminal representations. Preserve history selection, embeddings, backbone, scorer, loss and optimizer; reproduce the readout in the predictor writer within each dataset's single model."
}
